From 3a5322f026d2e029c969d80280ddde93f483000d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 08:33:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Report callback selectors in unentered member bodies as
 references

A callback selector written in a method body the analysis never entered
(`after idle [list [self] tick]`, `socket -server [namespace code [list
my connect]] $port`) was only a rename hazard, so references to the
method missed every callback site. Read-only reports may use written
words with registry and class facts for such a site; edits stay strict
(naming burn-down decision).

`references::unentered_callback_reference_spans` scans the declaring
class's member bodies and those of subclasses whose source order reaches
the declaration without an override. `[self] NAME` follows each
receiver's own export/unexport; `my NAME` does not. In an unentered body
a literal `my` head that the current context resolves to the
self-dispatch command names the current object's method, unless the
document defines, renames or aliases that command. A prefix wrapper
(`namespace code`) is followed from the sink that consumes it, never
scanned as a sink itself. The spans join `readonly_local_reference_report`
only; rename keeps `unproved_callback_method_selector` as its hazard.

The tcllib httpd `connect` callback (lines 1011 and 1014) and the cat
fixture's `Post` now resolve.

Closes #1956

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017u5g3HwS13Bq9AgkSKJ68y
